Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "commonwealth-african-reserve-system": britishAfricaGovernanceArticle({
    title:"Commonwealth African Reserve System",
    category:"Humanitarian administration",
    eyebrow:"Relief and mobilization network · from 1955",
    infoboxTitle:"Commonwealth African Reserve System",
    landscape:britishAfricaNetworkDiagram,
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 -->Refugees fleeing German Mittelafrika placed severe food, administrative, and social pressure on British territories in West, East, and Central Africa. Commonwealth governments were themselves economically strained, so their assistance could not become a cheap or free substitute for local provision.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <strong>Commonwealth African Reserve System</strong> is the accepted name for a reconstructed mid-1950s network joining paid Dominion supplies, British shipping, African ports, territorial administration, relief work, and strategic mobilization.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 -->The refugee emergency, the affected British territories' lack of resources, the variation in local administrative behavior, Britain's fear of losing strategic African possessions, and the importance of the Cape route and African resources are directly established. The author also establishes that Commonwealth economies could not sustain generous burden-sharing and specifically permits Canadian food and trucks only at real cost.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Reserve System's name, 1955–1956 formation, wider Dominion contribution schedules, port network, statutory integration, contractual machinery, dual-use character, abuses, and institutional survival are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Charter, headquarters, appropriations, permanent staff, depot roster, tonnages, command relationships, and later reorganizations remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins in the frontier emergency",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes that refugee inflows from German territory exceeded the food, housing, sanitation, transport, and administrative capacity of several British African possessions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Butler government's conversion of emergency Commonwealth shipments into a standing reserve and allocation system is accepted institutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"contributions",title:"Limited Commonwealth contributions",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes that Canada may provide grain, flour, canned goods, and trucks, but cannot provide them cheaply or as free aid; the other Commonwealth economies face comparable domestic constraints.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The fuller division of supply roles below is accepted reconstruction rather than a fixed shipment ledger.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Participant</th><th>Principal contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Canada</td><td>Grain, flour, canned food, trucks, railway equipment, medicines, and temporary buildings</td></tr><tr><td>Australia</td><td>Wheat, preserved meat, blankets, vehicles, agricultural machinery, and engineers</td></tr><tr><td>New Zealand</td><td>Dairy concentrates, meat, medical personnel, and veterinary support</td></tr><tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>Shipping, finance, administrators, field hospitals, communications, pumps, and railway equipment</td></tr><tr><td>South Africa</td><td>Maize, rail capacity, mine employment, port throughput, and security assistance</td></tr><tr><td>African territories</td><td>Land, local administration, labor, policing, reception sites, and political consent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Favorable contracts, guaranteed purchases, local taxation, emergency levies, and conditional grants are compatible financing mechanisms.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact prices and allocations remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"ports",title:"Accepted ports and reserve nodes",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 --><p>Freetown, Takoradi, Lagos, Mombasa, Dar es Salaam, Cape Town, Durban, and Portuguese Beira form the accepted receiving and transshipment chain, with interior railway depots and guarded road yards linking ocean supply to frontier relief.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its overlap with the Cape and Indian Ocean routes required for war, fuel, minerals, and reinforcement is compatible strategic interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact depot locations and traffic volumes remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"administration",title:"Administration and territorial consent",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes that African administrations differed in their willingness to follow London and that Britain considered leaving substantial management to local authorities.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Procurement by London and Dominion ministries, followed by distribution through governors, African ministers, chiefs, councils, churches, and missions, is accepted administrative reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The resulting pressure for local control over land, camps, employment, and resettlement is compatible development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"act",title:"Relationship to the Refugee and Frontier Act",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 --><p>The institutional pairing is accepted: the <a href='african-refugee-frontier-act.html'>African Refugee and Frontier Act</a> provides legal status, reception districts, permits, work schemes, and saturation powers, while the Reserve System provides material supply and transport.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The exact legal and budgetary relationship between the two remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"dual-use",title:"Relief and mobilization",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 --><p>The dual use of warehouses, vehicle parks, medical stores, communications, rail sidings, port improvements, and fuel arrangements for both civilian relief and military mobilization is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Official claims of economical preparedness and criticism of an imperial security grid are compatible political interpretations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"failures",title:"Shortage, patronage, and abuse",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 --><p>Uneven allocation favoring major ports and mining districts, together with theft, ration patronage, spoilage, coerced work, discriminatory screening, and land conflict, is compatible extrapolation from the established emergency and administrative constraints.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact incidents, investigations, and outcomes remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Institutional legacy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c7n2k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:00:00-06:00 --><p>Survival of the network as a Commonwealth stockpile, disaster-relief, transport, and mobilization mechanism is accepted institutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its connection to the <a href='east-african-defence-development-commission.html'>East African Defence and Development Commission</a> and <a href='defensive-trusteeship.html'>defensive trusteeship</a> is compatible synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The chronology and form of later reorganizations remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...britishAfricaGovernanceRelated,{href:"east-african-defence-development-commission.html",kicker:"Regional administrator",label:"East African Defence and Development Commission"},{href:"defensive-trusteeship.html",kicker:"Political justification",label:"Defensive Trusteeship"},{href:"cape-route.html",kicker:"Strategic geography",label:"Cape Route"}],
    facts:[["Working formation","1955–1956 emergency"],["Accepted character","Distributed reserve and allocation network"],["Established financing limit","Paid supply, not free relief"],["Proposed fields","Food, medicine, transport, shelter, and communications"],["Accepted routes","Atlantic–Cape–Indian Ocean"],["Accepted legal partner","African Refugee and Frontier Act"],["Accepted secondary function","Commonwealth mobilization"],["Formal charter","Open"]]
  }),

  "east-african-defence-development-commission": britishAfricaGovernanceArticle({
    title:"East African Defence and Development Commission",
    category:"Commonwealth institution",
    eyebrow:"East African common services · expanded 1958–1960",
    infoboxTitle:"East African Defence and Development Commission",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 -->The author establishes severe refugee and social pressure in Uganda and Tanganyika, German interest in recovering Tanganyika, and Britain's strategic concern for its East African possessions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <strong>East African Defence and Development Commission</strong> is the accepted institutional form through which Kenya, Uganda, and Tanganyika deepen common services while retaining separate territorial governments.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 -->The East African refugee emergency, German territorial pressure, the strategic value of British East Africa, and differing local willingness to follow London are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Deepened common services, the named Commission, its 1958–1960 expansion, High Commission ancestry, civil and security functions, British supervision, anti-federal safeguards, and separate territorial governments are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Final title, statute, headquarters, membership rules, budgets, departments, voting rules, and later constitutional development remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Accepted High Commission inheritance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 --><p>Survival of common railway, harbor, postal, telecommunications, and technical services after Britain's defeat, and their expansion between 1958 and 1960 into defense, emergency supply, and strategic development, are accepted institutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The exact legal succession from the East African High Commission remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"members",title:"Three territories, separate governments",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 --><p>Kenya, Uganda, and Tanganyika retaining distinct legislatures, executives, police systems, land politics, and constitutional trajectories under a common-services system is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Ugandan defense of kingdom institutions, Tanganyikan resistance to indefinite delay, and African opposition to Kenyan settler dominance are compatible political reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact constitutional arrangements remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"mandate",title:"Accepted civil-development mandate",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 --><p>Coordination of railways, ports, aviation, communications, disease surveillance, veterinary controls, strategic food reserves, engineering standards, and selected development procurement is the accepted civil mandate.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Departmental division, budgets, and enforcement powers remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"security",title:"Accepted frontier and refugee security",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 --><p>Common registers, intelligence exchange, transport controls, disarmament procedures, communications, procurement, and readiness recommendations are accepted security functions.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Dependence on territorial police, local forces, and British commands for execution is compatible administrative reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Operational command relationships remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"kenya",title:"The Kenyan contest",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author identifies Kenya and Tanganyika as administrations more willing than Rhodesia to follow London, while noting the risk of angering British colonists in Kenya.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Settler demands for Rhodesian-style discretion and London's refusal to create a Nairobi-controlled settler federation are accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Specific organizations, proposals, and confrontations remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"uganda",title:"Uganda and the kingdom question",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes that refugee inflows caused severe social strain in Uganda and that the territory contained strategically important resources.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The persistence of Bugandan and other kingdom institutions, concern over the Kilembe district, and a requirement for territorial accommodation in Commission decisions are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Specific voting and veto arrangements remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"tanganyika",title:"Tanganyika and the German frontier",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes serious refugee strain in Tanganyika and a German desire to recover the former colony, although German action is not certain.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> African local government under British external defense, with territorial police, retained bases, and common regional services, is accepted constitutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Nationalist criticism that the German threat indefinitely postpones sovereignty is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Institutional significance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h8p4k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T16:30:00-06:00 --><p>The Commission's reconciliation of territorial self-government with a shared Commonwealth strategic system is accepted institutional synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its illustration of <a href='defensive-trusteeship.html'>defensive trusteeship</a>'s contradiction—greater governing capacity paired with a stronger case against withdrawal—is compatible historical interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...britishAfricaGovernanceRelated,{href:"commonwealth-african-reserve-system.html",kicker:"Emergency supply partner",label:"Commonwealth African Reserve System"},{href:"no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine.html",kicker:"Withdrawal doctrine",label:"No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine"},{href:"associated-crown-state.html",kicker:"Constitutional framework",label:"Associated Crown State"}],
    facts:[["Working expansion","1958–1960"],["Accepted predecessor","East African High Commission"],["Accepted territories","Kenya, Uganda, and Tanganyika"],["Proposed civil remit","Transport, health, food, aviation, and communications"],["Proposed security remit","Registration, intelligence, and procurement"],["Accepted federal government","None"],["Final statutory title","Open"]]
  }),

  "salisbury-understanding": britishAfricaGovernanceArticle({
    title:"Salisbury Understanding",
    category:"Political agreement",
    eyebrow:"Unwritten British–Rhodesian settlement · late 1950s",
    infoboxTitle:"Salisbury Understanding",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 -->The author establishes a tacit late-Commonwealth practice under which Rhodesia receives broad domestic latitude while remaining aligned with the Commonwealth, sterling, and major British strategic decisions, with London avoiding detailed responsibility for local methods.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <strong>Salisbury Understanding</strong> as an unwritten late-1950s bargain covering frontier security, copper, rail traffic, Crown loyalty, and restraint toward Mittelafrika is accepted institutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 -->Rhodesian domestic discretion, continuing Commonwealth alignment, British strategic oversight, and metropolitan distance from local policy are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Salisbury Understanding name, late-1950s timing, unwritten form, transport obligations, security permissions, red lines, enforcement, and effect on separatism are accepted or compatible development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Negotiators, date, memoranda, territory, law, operations, and termination or replacement remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"background",title:"Background",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 --><p>Southern Rhodesian institutions, Northern Rhodesian copper, Nyasaland's labor and geography, and Cape railways have inherited foundations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Their organization as a Central African barrier against Mittelafrika, under refugee and sabotage pressure and seeking relief from case-by-case London approval, is accepted postwar reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"terms",title:"Working terms",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 --><blockquote>Maintain the frontier. Keep copper and rail traffic moving. Do not invade German territory. Avoid a scandal requiring parliamentary intervention.</blockquote><p>This is an archive paraphrase of the accepted working bargain, not an established treaty text.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Appointments, finance, intelligence liaison, security correspondence, and selective noninterference as its instruments are compatible institutional detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"permissions",title:"Security discretion",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 --><p>Pass systems, border camps, closed zones, armed farm and mine guards, preventive detention, population screening, relocation near roads, and policing of refugee organizations are compatible security practices under the reconstructed bargain.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact authorization and use remain open.</p><p>Local treatment of silence as permission and metropolitan treatment of silence as non-ownership are political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"red-lines",title:"British limits",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 --><p>Proposed prohibitions on mass execution, racial expulsion, unauthorized raids, annexation, and public Crown defiance are compatible red-line reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Their existence, wording, enforcement, and relationship to press and diplomatic risk remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"knowledge",title:"Ministerial distance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes that British politics deliberately avoids detailed ownership of South African, Rhodesian, and Kenyan domestic methods while retaining strategic expectations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Intelligence and colonial reporting focused on strategic effects while operational detail remained in Salisbury is compatible administrative reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><p>Its later relationship to <a href='late-commonwealth-dont-ask-culture.html'>late-Commonwealth “don't ask” culture</a> is accepted continuity.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Political effects",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 --><p>Rhodesian belief that local authorities bore frontier risks while London retained moral claims and the option of abandonment is compatible political reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Stronger Crown loyalty than confidence in Parliament, and its role in later separatism, remain accepted interpretation rather than fixed constitutional fact.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"opposition",title:"Opposition",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 --><p>Opposition by Common Wealth, Liberals, churches, African representatives, and parts of Labour, including demands for camp inspection, judicial review, published agreements, African land control, and constitutional timetables, is accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact campaigns, votes, and leaders remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-s4q9k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:10:00-06:00 --><p>The Understanding as an early model of strategically retained and domestically devolved empire is accepted institutional interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Claims that it strengthened Rhodesia while weakening Westminster's long-term control are likewise interpretive.</p><p>Post-Accra use by defenders of security and critics of settler autonomy is compatible political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...britishAfricaGovernanceRelated,{href:"rhodesia.html",kicker:"Principal beneficiary",label:"Rhodesia"},{href:"defensive-trusteeship.html",kicker:"Wider doctrine",label:"Defensive Trusteeship"},{href:"late-commonwealth-dont-ask-culture.html",kicker:"Later political practice",label:"Late-Commonwealth “Don't Ask” Culture"}],
    facts:[["Working date","Late 1950s"],["Working form","Unwritten political understanding"],["Proposed British party","United Kingdom government"],["Proposed local party","Rhodesian and federal authorities"],["Proposed objects","Frontier, copper, railways, and Crown loyalty"],["Proposed limits","No unauthorized war or parliamentary crisis"],["Formal text","None established"]]
  }),

  "no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine": britishAfricaGovernanceArticle({
    title:"No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine",
    category:"Imperial doctrine",
    eyebrow:"British withdrawal doctrine · articulated 1956",
    infoboxTitle:"No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 -->The author establishes that Britain seeks to retain its African possessions and fears that withdrawal from exposed territories would permit rapid German occupation or client rule.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <strong>No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine</strong> as a 1956 Butler-government rule conditioning sovereignty on government, security, defensible borders, exclusion of hostile bases, Commonwealth access, and protection from rival occupation is accepted doctrinal reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 -->Britain's post-defeat imperial retention and fear of German entry into vacated African territories are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The doctrine's name, 1956 articulation, maxim, six conditions, Butlerite authorship, relationship to defensive trusteeship and the Accra Compact, party criticism, circularity, and post-Accra reinforcement are accepted or compatible development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Author, speech, Cabinet paper, legal force, wording, territorial tests, and exceptions remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"maxim",title:"Working maxim",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 --><blockquote>Britain will not evacuate a government into a vacuum and call the vacuum freedom.</blockquote><p>The maxim and its reference to civil war, rival provisional governments, foreign security assistance, and absorption are accepted doctrinal formulation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No direct authorial wording or first use is established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"conditions",title:"Working conditions for withdrawal",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 --><ol><li>A recognized local government exists.</li><li>Its police and armed forces can maintain order.</li><li>Its borders are defensible.</li><li>Hostile foreign bases are excluded by treaty.</li><li>Strategic ports and communications remain available to the Commonwealth.</li><li>No immediate rival occupation is expected.</li></ol><p>These six cumulative tests are accepted policy reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Their precise wording, legal status, and application remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"butler",title:"Butlerite interpretation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 --><p>Presentation of the doctrine as an African form of Butler's Contract Commonwealth, transferring domestic authority through agreements while retaining defense obligations, is accepted political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its authorship and relationship to Cabinet policy remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"applications",title:"Applications",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 --><p>Application through Associated Crown States, East African common services, a Southern Cameroons frontier territory, retained bases, local armies, and the <a href='salisbury-understanding.html'>Salisbury Understanding</a> is compatible institutional reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact instruments and territory-by-territory tests remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"circularity",title:"Readiness criticism",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 --><p>Criticism that Britain controlled military development, declared local forces inadequate, and used that inadequacy to retain control is accepted political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The same critique applies when strategic-access tests make readiness contingent on continued British power.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"party",title:"Party positions",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 --><p>Accepted party reconstruction assigns Conservatives an emphasis on government, development, defense, and succession, and the Union Party a preference for Commonwealth integration. It leaves Labour divided, associates Common Wealth and Liberal critics with demands for oversight, and gives Powell a treaty-based particularism.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact speeches, votes, and policy texts remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"accra",title:"Accra test",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 --><p>The 1963 Ghana collapse and later <a href='accra-clause.html'>Accra Clause</a> are accepted setting chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Interpretation of the collapse as vindication of No Empty Flagstaffs, and of the Clause as its treaty successor, is compatible political analysis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"reputation",title:"Reception",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-e5r0k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T15:30:00-06:00 --><p>Supporters' description of the doctrine as responsible protection and opponents' description of it as an indefinitely renewable imperial test are accepted political interpretations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Their institutional reception and later influence remain open in detail.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...britishAfricaGovernanceRelated,{href:"defensive-trusteeship.html",kicker:"Governing philosophy",label:"Defensive Trusteeship"},{href:"crown-frontier-territory.html",kicker:"Frontier application",label:"Crown Frontier Territory"},{href:"accra-clause.html",kicker:"Later treaty form",label:"Accra Clause"}],
    facts:[["Working articulation","1956"],["Proposed government","Butler Conservative ministry"],["Working scope","British and Commonwealth territorial withdrawal"],["Proposed core test","Viable government before flag withdrawal"],["Proposed reservations","Bases, communications, and external defense"],["Accepted criticism","Circular readiness standard"],["Working successor","Accra Clause"]]
  }),

  "crown-frontier-territory": britishAfricaGovernanceArticle({
    title:"Crown Frontier Territory",
    category:"Constitutional status",
    eyebrow:"Commonwealth frontier status · from 1957",
    infoboxTitle:"Crown Frontier Territory",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 -->The author establishes that Southern Cameroons faced refugee pressure beyond its resources and that British withdrawal might be followed within days by German occupation or a German-backed administration.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A <strong>Crown Frontier Territory</strong>, with Southern Cameroons as its principal African example, is the accepted constitutional response: internal autonomy beneath retained British defense and strategic powers.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 -->Southern Cameroons' refugee and food pressure, inadequate local resources, exposure to German Cameroon, and risk of rapid German absorption after withdrawal are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its post-Accra frontier status, separation from Nigeria, assembly, citizenship, British defense guarantee, Nigerian economic ties, constabulary, refugee districts, permanent facilities, and relation to Kribi are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Whether Crown Frontier Territory was a uniform legal category, together with its formal title, flag, capital, borders, officeholders, electoral system, and later constitutional development, remains open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"purpose",title:"Accepted purpose",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes the danger of strategic withdrawal from Southern Cameroons.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A status separating domestic institutions from retained external defense is the accepted constitutional answer for exposed territories.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its use beyond Southern Cameroons remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"southern-cameroons",title:"Southern Cameroons",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author's fixed premises are that Southern Cameroons remains British-held during the refugee emergency, lacks resources for the inflow, and is dangerously exposed to German intervention.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its separation from Nigeria, local assembly and citizenship, executive, courts, domestic powers, and continuing Nigerian trade and transport links are accepted constitutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact instruments and institutional design remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"reserved",title:"Accepted Crown powers",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 --><p>British retention of defense, external relations, strategic communications, air access, intelligence installations, and emergency reinforcement is accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> An enlarged constabulary and locally recruited forces beneath British external command are compatible security reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Force structure and command agreements remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"frontier",title:"The German frontier",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 --><p>Direct canon places German-controlled territory across the frontier and makes rapid German intervention a British concern.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The neighboring Kribi aerospace and missile complex, together with a heavily watched 1960s boundary of signals stations, patrol roads, airfields, refugee districts, and surveillance posts, is accepted strategic development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact facilities and deployment dates remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"citizenship",title:"Citizenship and movement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 --><p>Local citizenship without an automatic right of settlement in Britain, together with permit control in refugee districts and strategic zones under the African Refugee and Frontier Act, is accepted legal reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The resulting combination of local political identity and dense security jurisdiction is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"comparison",title:"Relation to Associated Crown States",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 --><p>The frontier territory's shared domestic self-government and local citizenship with an <a href='associated-crown-state.html'>Associated Crown State</a>, but stronger British defense and intelligence presence, is accepted constitutional synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Whether this was a distinct legal category or merely an Accra settlement adapted to local conditions remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Local political dispute",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 --><p>Support for a Crown guarantee against German absorption and nationalist criticism of a permanent listening-post regime are compatible political interpretations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Division among Nigerian federation, separate Cameroonian identity, and negotiated cross-border relations is accepted political reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Parties, leaders, elections, and exact programs remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Institutional significance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-f2w7k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T17:30:00-06:00 --><p>Southern Cameroons as the clearest territorial expression of the <a href='no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine.html'>No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine</a> is accepted institutional synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Judging the settlement by the coexistence of local government, British access, and frontier stability rather than by an independence date is compatible contemporary interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...britishAfricaGovernanceRelated,{href:"no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine.html",kicker:"Founding doctrine",label:"No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine"},{href:"associated-crown-state.html",kicker:"Related constitutional form",label:"Associated Crown State"},{href:"mittelafrika.html",kicker:"Neighboring imperial system",label:"Mittelafrika"}],
    facts:[["Working establishment","From 1957"],["Accepted principal example","Southern Cameroons"],["Proposed domestic institutions","Assembly, executive, courts, and local citizenship"],["Accepted external defense","British guarantee"],["Proposed security institutions","Constabulary, air, signals, and intelligence facilities"],["Accepted economic connection","Nigeria"],["Formal uniform category","Open"]]
  }),

  "defensive-trusteeship": britishAfricaGovernanceArticle({
    title:"Defensive trusteeship",
    category:"Political doctrine",
    eyebrow:"British African doctrine · 1950s onward",
    infoboxTitle:"Defensive trusteeship",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 -->The author establishes a British African order in which strategic necessity overrides moral scruple, London delegates coercive frontier administration, and German rule supplies the recurrent lesser-evil defense for retaining unequal imperial power.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> <strong>Defensive trusteeship</strong> is the accepted name for this doctrine, commonly summarized as controlled refuge, accelerated local government, and retained imperial defense.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 -->Britain's unaffordable moral scruple, strategic refusal to abandon African possessions, growing concession to Rhodesian and South African methods, delegation of justice to local administrations, acceptance of harsher punishment, unequal but dignified particularism, civilizing-mission revival, Powell's advocacy, and comparison with German rule are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The term defensive trusteeship, three-part formula, institutional roster, detailed abuses, party taxonomy, opposition label, and post-Accra succession are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> First use, official text, uniform legal definition, territorial membership, and final endpoint remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"formula",title:"Accepted three-part formula",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 --><blockquote>Controlled refuge. Accelerated local government. Retained imperial defense.</blockquote><p>The formula and its rejection of an open frontier, direct gubernatorial rule without development, and immediate strategic withdrawal are accepted doctrinal synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> No direct authorial wording, speech, or official adoption is established.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"strategic",title:"Strategic argument",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes that Britain cannot afford to lose its African possessions, that their resources and the wider strategic position matter, and that withdrawal may yield rapid German occupation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Treating every undefended port, railway, and provisional government as part of Atlantic–Cape–Indian Ocean route security is compatible defense-policy synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact plans and priority lists remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"humanitarian",title:"Humanitarian claim",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 --><p>The refugee danger and Britain's moral scruple about abandoning vulnerable people are directly established, as is the country's inability to finance a generous Commonwealth relief system.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Claims that food, clinics, missions, transport, and protected districts saved lives, and that local ministries gained authority because Whitehall could not administer camps and settlement, are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact humanitarian outcomes remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"institutions",title:"Accepted institutional expression",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 --><p>The <a href='african-refugee-frontier-act.html'>African Refugee and Frontier Act</a>, <a href='commonwealth-african-reserve-system.html'>Commonwealth African Reserve System</a>, <a href='no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine.html'>No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine</a>, <a href='accra-compact.html'>Accra Compact</a>, East African common services, and territorial security bargains are accepted institutional expressions of the doctrine.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Their coordination under a single formal program remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"government",title:"Governmental particularism",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes a particularist order presented as unequal but dignified, with London increasingly accepting that African territories cannot all be governed like Britain.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The coexistence of Associated Crown States, federations, protected kingdoms, frontier territories, settler governments, colonies, and base agreements is accepted constitutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Criticism that this variety lets London preserve strategic control is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"abuses",title:"Emergency law and abuse",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes devolved local justice, punishments harsher than London prefers, administrative evasion, and a practical culture in which scruple follows perceived necessity.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Permanent camps, selective closure, coerced or underpaid work, preventive detention, relocation, censorship, unequal nationality, and security concessions are accepted manifestations.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact laws, cases, and territorial frequency remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"politics",title:"British and African politics",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author assigns Enoch Powell a leading role in negotiating morally with white African arguments and reviving a civilizing mission without a clear endpoint.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Conservative contractual defenses, Union Commonwealth planning, Labour division, Liberal and Common Wealth oversight demands, church criticism, African nationalism, Japanese charges of hypocrisy, and visible local government as London's reply are accepted party and propaganda reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact speeches and alignments remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"contradiction",title:"The permanent contradiction",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-m6x1k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T18:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes the tragedy: Britain faces a real German danger and genuine administrative crisis, yet uses necessity to concede methods it considers morally compromised and to retain colonies it cannot afford to abandon.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The claim that institutions built for African government also strengthen the case for Commonwealth protection is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Survival after Ghana through Ordered Particularism and the Accra Clause is accepted later development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[...britishAfricaGovernanceRelated,{href:"no-empty-flagstaff-doctrine.html",kicker:"Withdrawal rule",label:"No Empty Flagstaff Doctrine"},{href:"salisbury-understanding.html",kicker:"Settler bargain",label:"Salisbury Understanding"},{href:"ordered-particularism.html",kicker:"Post-Accra successor",label:"Ordered Particularism"}],
    facts:[["Working development","Mid-1950s"],["Accepted core formula","Controlled refuge, local government, retained defense"],["Established strategic premise","African retention against German advance"],["Accepted constitutional instrument","Accra Compact"],["Accepted withdrawal doctrine","No Empty Flagstaffs"],["Proposed opposition description","Empire under emergency law"],["Final endpoint","Open"]]
  })
});

function appendBritishAfricaGovernance(id,key,items){
  const article=window.deepArticles[id];
  if(!article)return;
  article[key]=article[key]||[];
  for(const item of items){
    if(!article[key].some(existing=>(existing.id&&existing.id===item.id)||(existing.href&&existing.href===item.href))) article[key].push(item);
  }
}

